<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Machynlleth</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Machynlleth is a market town in Powys, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wales">Wales</a>, in the Dyfi valley. In 1404 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/owain-glyndwr">Owain Glyndŵr</a> held a parliament there, where he is said to have been crowned <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/prince-of-wales">Prince of Wales</a> in the presence of envoys from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-castile">Castile</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/scotland">Scotland</a>. It is sometimes called the ancient capital of Wales.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/machynlleth.jpg" alt="Machynlleth" width="320" height="213" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Celuici · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Central_Machynlleth,_June_2016.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
